TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42968811
TI  - Baseline monocyte GIP receptor mRNA expression is associated with reductions in body weight and FIB-4 index after switching to tirzepatide in patients with type 2 diabetes.
AU  - Okuma Hideyuki
JO  - Diabetology international
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1007/s13340-026-00940-3
AB  - Tirzepatide improves body weight and glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes, but factors associated with inter-individual differences after switching remain unclear. We examined whether baseline monocyte glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide receptor (GIPR) mRNA expression was associated with changes in body weight and the fibrosis-4 (FIB-4) index after switching from glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) to tirzepatide. This single-center retrospective study included 90 patients with type 2 diabetes who had received a GLP-1RA for at least 6 months before switching. Baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was measured by quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction. Associations with percentage body-weight change and FIB-4 index change during the 6 months after tirzepatide initiation were assessed by correlation and multivariable regression. Exploratory ex vivo assays were performed to assess monocyte responses to native GIP. Higher baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was associated with greater reductions in body weight and the FIB-4 index and remained significant in multivariable models. The association with the FIB-4 index remained significant after additional adjustment for percentage body-weight change at 6 months after tirzepatide initiation. GIPR mRNA expression was not associated with body-weight change during the preceding 6 months of GLP-1RA therapy. Native GIP suppressed lipopolysaccharide-induced interleukin-6 and tumor necrosis factor-alpha responses in monocytes. Baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was associated with body-weight and FIB-4 index reductions after switching to tirzepatide. It may represent an exploratory marker of clinical changes, but treatment specificity, causal GIPR-dependent mechanisms, and structural liver fibrosis regression were not established. Summary of the study design and main finding. Baseline GIPR mRNA expression in peripheral blood monocytes was associated with reductions in body weight and the FIB-4 index after switching to tirzepatide in patients with type 2 diabetes. In exploratory ex vivo assays, native GIP suppressed LPS-induced inflammatory responses, supporting monocyte GIPR expression as a candidate exploratory marker requiring further validation. The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s13340-026-00940-3.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42968811/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42986421
TI  - Rethinking endpoints for anti-obesity pharmacotherapy in patients with hr+ early breast cancer receiving endocrine therapy: from body weight and BMI to body composition.
AU  - Lampropoulou Dimitra Ioanna
AU  - Dedes Vasileios
AU  - Pliakou Evangelia
AU  - Nasi Despoina
AU  - Michaelidou Iris Aliki
AU  - Kamperaki Sophia
AU  - Panoutsopoulos Georgios
JO  - Expert review of clinical pharmacology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1080/17512433.2026.2761106
AB  - Body weight (BW) and body mass index (BMI) are commonly used to assess obesity and response to weight-management interventions in patients with hormone receptor-positive (HR+) early breast cancer (eBC) receiving endocrine therapy. However, these measures do not adequately reflect changes in fat mass, visceral adiposity, lean mass and sarcopenia-related phenotypes. This focused narrative review examines whether BW and BMI are sufficient endpoints for evaluating obesity management and anti-obesity pharmacotherapy in patients with HR+ eBC receiving endocrine therapy. PubMed and ClinicalTrials.gov were searched for relevant evidence until 23 June 2026, including BC-specific studies of obesity pharmacotherapy and registered interventional trials. Studies have reported increases in total and visceral fat mass after adjuvant BC treatment, while changes in lean mass have been less consistent. The identified BC-specific studies remain mainly retrospective and weight-centric, with none assessing body composition as a key outcome. BW and BMI may be useful entry and monitoring variables, but insufficient as stand-alone endpoints. Body composition should be considered as a complementary endpoint, alongside metabolic, functional and oncologic outcomes. Future studies should also assess endocrine therapy adherence and persistence to better characterize the benefits and risks of anti-obesity pharmacotherapy in patients with HR+ eBC.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42986421/
