TI  - Antidiabetic Therapy and Atrial Fibrillation: Pathophysiological Links, Therapeutic Implications, and Evidence-Based Outcomes.
AU  - Zisis Marios
AU  - Cheiletzaris Theofanis
AU  - Pieper Orestis Filippos
AU  - Kotsovelou Vasiliki
AU  - Spartalis Kyriakos
AU  - Pechlivanidis Sotiris
AU  - Mystakidi Vasiliki Chara
AU  - Katsarou Ourania
AU  - Gialafos Elias
AU  - Theofilis Panagiotis
AU  - Oikonomou Evangelos
AU  - Siasos Gerasimos
JO  - Current cardiology reviews
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2174/011573403X487530260806114153
AB  - Diabetes Mellitus (DM) is strongly associated with an increased risk, severity, and recurrence of Atrial Fibrillation (AF). This narrative review aims to synthesize the mechanistic links between DM and AF and evaluate the impact of major antidiabetic drug categories on AF incidence, burden, and post-ablation outcomes. A focused narrative review of the literature was conducted using predefined eligibility criteria for study selection and specific keywords to search major scientific databases and grey literature. Hyperglycemia, oxidative stress, epicardial adipose tissue expansion, autonomic imbalance, and atrial remodeling represent core pathophysiological mechanisms connecting DM and AF. Among antidiabetic pharmacotherapies, the glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist semaglutide displays a potential to reduce AF incidence. Sodium-glucose transporter 2 inhibitors have similarly been shown to lower the incidence of AF in most of the available evidence, while offering an emerging, preliminary benefit for post-ablation success. Metformin also demonstrates a positive impact across both primary and secondary AF prevention, albeit with less robust evidence. Other incretin therapies, including tirzepatide, exhibit neutral effects, while thiazolidinediones and dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitors yield conflicting results. Data from insulin and sulfonylureas suggest an unfavorable risk profile, though potentially confounded by indication. Antidiabetic therapy selection may have clinically meaningful implications for AF risk modification and rhythm-control outcomes. Weight-centric and cardio-protective strategies, particularly SGLT2 inhibitors and semaglutide, alongside the traditional option of metformin, show promising signals. Nevertheless, available data derive largely from unadjudicated adverseevent reporting and observational cohorts, currently precluding definitive class-wide or agentspecific recommendations. Incorporating atrial fibrillation risk into the individualization of antidiabetic therapy is a promising and clinically relevant strategy. However, establishing any specific agent as a preferred option for AF management remains premature in the absence of dedicated trials with primary AF endpoints.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42983209/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42979856
TI  - Tirzepatide for the treatment of obstructive sleep apnea: from apnea-hypopnea index reduction to cardiometabolic risk mitigation-a narrative review.
AU  - Mesfin-Bireda Samrawit
AU  - Hillman Urek Katherine
JO  - Multidisciplinary respiratory medicine
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.5826/mrm.2026.1137
AB  - Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a chronic respiratory disorder with nocturnal oxygen desaturation that is often associated with multisystemic sequelae such as hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, and increased morbidity and mortality. Obesity has been proposed as a modifiable risk factor; hence, the recent shift in focus to investigating anti-obesity medications for the management of OSA. Tirzepatide is the first U.S. FDA-approved pharmacotherapy for the treatment of moderate-to-severe OSA in adults with obesity to be utilized in combination with low caloric intake and physical activity, given its prior success in managing type 2 diabetes (T2D) and obesity. This narrative review evaluates tirzepatide and the SURMOUNT-OSA trial program as the key contemporary anchors for the pharmacological treatment of OSA in adults with concurrent obesity. Because the randomized evidence base currently comprises a single pivotal trial program, the review centers on SURMOUNT-OSA and its prespecified endpoints along with post hoc analyses, supplemented by observational, modeling, and mechanistic studies. In the SURMOUNT-OSA program, tirzepatide produced randomized, placebo-controlled reductions in the apnea-hypopnea index, sleep apnea-specific hypoxic burden, body weight, and systemic inflammation. The program was designed around respiratory and metabolic endpoints rather than clinical events, so it cannot support conclusions about mortality, cardiovascular, or renal outcomes. Favorable signals for those events, including all-cause mortality, major adverse kidney events, and major adverse cardiovascular events, come only from nonrandomized observational cohorts and are best regarded as directional and exploratory rather than definitive. Tirzepatide may help frame OSA as a multifactorial and partially metabolically treatable disease and may represent a clinically meaningful expansion of treatment options alongside, rather than as a replacement for, established mechanical and procedural therapies. The available evidence is anchored in a single phase 3 trial program and limited by short follow-up and the absence of definitive comparative and long-term outcome data. Long-term durability of effect after discontinuation, generalizability beyond the SURMOUNT-OSA-eligible population, OSA-specific economic analysis, and head-to-head comparisons against CPAP remain the principal unsettled questions requiring dedicated long-term studies before firm clinical recommendations can be made.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42979856/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42977781
TI  - [New oral anti-obesity drugs: the future or second choice?].
AU  - Hauner H
JO  - Innere Medizin (Heidelberg, Germany)
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s00108-026-02204-3
AB  - Obesity and its comorbidities represent a growing worldwide health challenge. To date, lifestyle modification is the most frequently used treatment modality, but offers only limited success in terms of both the extent and maintenance of weight loss. Potent injectable incretin mimetics such as semaglutide and tirzepatide were recently introduced as novel pharmacological options for improved and clinically meaningful weight management. In 2026, the first two oral glucagon-like peptide‑1 (GLP-1)-based agents, namely oral semaglutide and orforglipron, were approved and are promoted as alternative drug choices for additional weight loss, allowing people with obesity to make their own choice from a broader spectrum of therapeutics. This review describes the current state of knowledge on these new medications for the treatment of obesity. A variety of other oral drugs is currently under development and will substantially expand obesity management in the coming years.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42977781/
