AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) have rapidly expanded in use for the treatment of obesity and type 2 diabetes, including among women of reproductive age. Emerging evidence suggests that GLP-1 signaling may influence reproductive physiology through central effects on the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis and indirect metabolic pathways. In women with polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS), GLP-1 RAs improve insulin resistance and androgen profiles and may improve menstrual regularity. Most GLP-1 RAs do not significantly affect oral contraceptive pharmacokinetics, although tirzepatide may require temporary backup contraception. Evidence regarding fertility outcomes and pregnancy safety remains limited.
