AB  - GLP-1 and dual GLP-1/GIP receptor agonists have improved obesity treatment, but their high cost requires economic evaluation to guide resource allocation. To assess the short-term cost-effectiveness of subcutaneous tirzepatide, semaglutide, and liraglutide for treating adults with overweight or obesity without diabetes in Iran. A decision-tree model was developed to compare tirzepatide, semaglutide, and liraglutide over 68 weeks in adults aged ≥ 18 years with BMI ≥ 30 kg/m² or ≥ 27 kg/m² with at least one additional weight-related complication, excluding those with diabetes, from the payer perspective. Clinical effectiveness, adverse event (AE), and treatment discontinuation data were obtained from published literatures. Quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) were estimated based on BMI changes and AE-related disutility. Direct medical costs included drug acquisition and AE management costs. Deterministic and probabilistic sensitivity analyses were performed. Compared with semaglutide, tirzepatide generated greater health benefits but at higher costs, resulting in an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio of US$34,423.89/QALY. Liraglutide was dominated by semaglutide. Semaglutide was cost-effective at the Iranian willingness-to-pay threshold of US$ (Purchasing power parity (PPP) 15,558.5/QALY, equal to one time GDP per capita in 2026. Sensitivity analyses confirmed the robustness of the findings. Semaglutide was the cost-effective injectable GLP-1-based therapy in Iran. Although tirzepatide provided greater health benefits, its higher cost limited its cost-effectiveness at current prices. The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s40200-026-02114-z.
